  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - We are to minimize the maximum overlap integral C5 for a step function h defined on [0,2]\n   - The current approach using 140 grid points found a value of 0.384236\n   - We aim to improve this by using a different representation and optimization strategy:\n        * Use a parent solution for initialization (if available)\n        * Employ a convex relaxation technique (with indicator constraints) to handle the non-convexity\n        * Use a two-phase optimization: (1) trust-region constraints and (2) nonlinear penalty function\n        * Consider scaling by increasing the resolution from 140 to 560 points (16 times finer)\n        * Maintain feasibility by designing projection operators for both integral and box constraints\n   - Limitations: increased dimensionality at higher resolution requires efficient constraint handling and optimization\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Representation: Piecewise constant function h(x) defined on grid points [j*dx, (j+1)*dx) for j=0 to n-1\n   - Variables: h = [h0, h1, ..., h_{n-1}], each 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n   - Objective: Minimize C5 = dx * max_k \u2211_{j} h[j] * (1 - h[k+j])   (mode='full' correlation)\n   - Constraints:\n        1. Integral: \u2211_{i} h[i]*dx = 1  (equivalent to sum(h) = n/2)\n        2. Box: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a) Parameter initialization:\n        n = 140 (base) or 560 (optional)\n        dx = 2.0 / n\n        integral_target = n / 2\n        x = np.arange(n) * dx  # sample points\n\n        If 'initial_h_values' in global scope:\n            start_point = initial_h_values (scaled if necessary)\n        Else:\n            start_point = np.full(n, 0.5)\n\n   b) Objective function:\n        def objective(h):\n            # Apply clipping and projection\n            cell = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n            cell_sum = np.sum(cell)\n            # Introduce penalization if sum(region) not close to target\n            return dx * max(np.correlate(cell, 1-cell, mode='full'))\n\n   c) Constraint and projection methods:\n        def constraint_func(h):\n            return np.sum(h) - integral_target   # (without projection)\n\n        def project_solution(h, target_sum):\n            # Scale to adjust sum\n            current_sum = np.sum(np.clip(h, 0, 1))\n            if abs(current_sum - target_sum) < 1e-5:\n                return\n            scale = target_sum / current_sum\n            h_clean = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n            h_clean = h_clean * scale\n            # Handle values >1 by crossing into adjacent intervals\n            for i in range(len(h_clean)):\n                if h_clean[i] > 1:\n                    h_clean[i] = 1\n   \n   d) Optimization phases:\n        Phase 1 (Constraint Force):\n            - Use projected gradient method with trust regions (scale factors)\n            - initialize from start_point\n            - Iteratively enforce integral constraint via projection\n\n        Phase 2 (Penalty):\n            - Use SLSQP with finite-difference gradients\n            - Add barrier terms to strictly enforce 0\u2264h\u22641\n            - tuned penalty weights\n\n   e) Retry different initial conditions:\n        - After 25% of time budget, generate new random points\n        - Use: uniform grid, random within bounds, sinusoidal variants, and perturbations\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   a) Primary resolution: 560 points (4x original)\n   b) Secondary: 140 points (original)\n   c) Optimization methods:\n        - CVXPY for convex relaxations\n        - scipy.optimize for nonlinear chem\n        - Random search for initialization\n        - Trust-region constrained phase\n        - Penalty phase\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   - Base implementation (140 points): ~30 seconds for multi-start optimization (5 alg variatns)\n   - Fine-resolution (560 points): ~120 penalty evaluations due to higher cost\n   - Monitoring: \n        - C5 improvement: if worst 5 candidates worsening, break\n        - Graceful degradation: binary search tree fallback\n\n6. Validation and interface:\n   - Direct computation: np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') with scaling\n   - Safety checks:\n        - sum(h) == (n_points/2) \u00b1 tolerance\n        - 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n        - finite and positive C5\n   - Git-like version tracking of parameters",
